From 9f9a400531252fc613e0faa538ba4545ef0a1e65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DeltaVector1 <DeltaVector1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 10:09:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 27/32] Compress the rollout tunnels; stop SSH killing live
 ones

78.4% of step 6's rollouts errored, and every signature was ReadTimeout. The
zero_advantage filter dropped 0.5%, gibberish 0.2%, truncation 0%. Rollout
throughput and rollout drop rate were never two problems: the transport is
both.

Compression=yes on all 24 rollout tunnels. The batch tunnel has had it since
the day it would not carry a batch at all, where it took throughput from under
130 KB/s to 4.9 MB/s. Rollout traffic is JSON holding generated text and
compresses just as well, and on a loss-limited path fewer bytes means
proportionally fewer lost packets.

Keepalive 10x3 (30s) -> 15x8 (120s). SSH keepalives are ordinary packets on a
link losing 10-33% of them, so three consecutive misses happen routinely on a
working tunnel, and under load they miss on every tunnel at once. SSH was
killing live tunnels and every request in flight on them. tunnel_watchdog.sh
now owns wedge detection from the n82 side, where it can see what SSH cannot,
so SSH should only reap a genuinely dead TCP path. Exactly one of the two may
be impatient.

Measured over equal windows, rolling two tunnels at a time to keep the batch:
ReadTimeouts 2.2/min -> 0.75/min, collection 9.65 -> 11.2 rollouts/min.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017wiESxhziDrnsLnG9aizTd
---
 scripts/h200/start_tunnel.sh | 31 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/scripts/h200/start_tunnel.sh b/scripts/h200/start_tunnel.sh
index 8d73dfb..7132bb7 100755
--- a/scripts/h200/start_tunnel.sh
+++ b/scripts/h200/start_tunnel.sh
@@ -5,11 +5,27 @@ remote_port=${1:?usage: start_tunnel.sh REMOTE_PORT LOCAL_PORT}
 local_port=${2:?usage: start_tunnel.sh REMOTE_PORT LOCAL_PORT}
 health_port=$((remote_port + 1000))
 
-# 10s x 3 = a dead tunnel is torn down in ~30s. It was 15 x 20 = 300s, and
-# over a link with 10-33% packet loss the wedged tunnels accumulated faster
-# than they were reaped: observed 22 of 24 dead with h200a taking zero traffic,
-# and later all 24 wedged at once. The n82-side watchdog probes for the wedges
-# SSH itself cannot see (forward alive, no data flowing).
+# Keepalive: 15s x 8 = a dead TCP path is reaped in ~120s.
+#
+# This value has now been set three ways, and each was right for the watchdog
+# that existed at the time. 15 x 20 (300s) was chosen when nothing else could
+# detect a wedge, and it let wedged tunnels pile up. 10 x 3 (30s) replaced it,
+# but SSH keepalives are ordinary packets on a link that loses 10-33% of them,
+# so three consecutive misses happen routinely on a tunnel that is working --
+# and under load they miss on every tunnel at once. SSH was then killing live
+# tunnels and every request in flight on them.
+#
+# scripts/china/tunnel_watchdog.sh now owns wedge detection from the n82 side,
+# where it can see the thing SSH cannot: forward alive, no data flowing. So
+# SSH's only job here is to notice a genuinely dead TCP path, and it should be
+# patient about it. Exactly one of the two may be impatient; right now neither
+# is, because a wedge is expensive to misdiagnose in both directions.
+#
+# Compression is the reason this link works at all. On the batch tunnel it took
+# throughput from under 130 KB/s to 4.9 MB/s. Rollout traffic is JSON holding
+# generated text, which compresses just as well, and on a loss-limited path
+# fewer bytes means proportionally fewer lost packets. It costs a little CPU on
+# hosts that have plenty.
 while true; do
   ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=30 china-trainer \
     "sudo ss -H -lntp 'sport = :$remote_port' | sed -n 's/.*pid=\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p' | xargs -r sudo kill; sudo ss -H -lntp 'sport = :$health_port' | sed -n 's/.*pid=\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p' | xargs -r sudo kill" || true
@@ -17,10 +33,11 @@ while true; do
     -o BatchMode=yes \
     -o ConnectTimeout=30 \
     -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes \
-    -o ServerAliveInterval=10 \
-    -o ServerAliveCountMax=3 \
+    -o ServerAliveInterval=15 \
+    -o ServerAliveCountMax=8 \
     -o TCPKeepAlive=yes \
     -o IPQoS=none \
+    -o Compression=yes \
     -R "127.0.0.1:$remote_port:127.0.0.1:$local_port" \
     -R "127.0.0.1:$health_port:127.0.0.1:8000" \
     china-trainer || true
